In C++, class objects are not automatically passed by reference to other functions. By default, when you pass an object to a function, it is passed by value. This means that a copy of the object is created, and the function operates on this copy rather than the original object. This is known as "pass-by-value."

Pass-by-value can have some downsides, such as increased memory usage and the potential for slower performance because the object must be copied. To avoid these issues, you can explicitly pass objects by reference using the '&' symbol. When passing by reference, the function operates on the original object directly, and no copy is created.

Here's a brief example to illustrate the difference between pass-by-value and pass-by-reference:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

class MyClass {
public:
    int value;

    MyClass(int v) : value(v) {}
};

void pass_by_value(MyClass obj) {
    obj.value = 10;
}

void pass_by_reference(MyClass &obj) {
    obj.value = 20;
}

int main() {
    MyClass obj1(5);
    MyClass obj2(5);

    pass_by_value(obj1);
    std::cout << "After pass_by_value: " << obj1.value << std::endl; // Output: 5

    pass_by_reference(obj2);
    std::cout << "After pass_by_reference: " << obj2.value << std::endl; // Output: 20

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, pass_by_value does not modify the original object's value because it operates on a copy of the object. However, pass_by_reference modifies the original object's value directly because it operates on the object itself.

Here's an implementation of the described HTTPS file server using Express.js in Node.js:

```const https = require('https');
const express = require('express');

const app = express();

const options = {
  key: fs.readFileSync('server.key'),
  cert: fs.readFileSync('server.crt')
};

const clients = new Set();

app.get('/:filename', (req, res) => {
  const { ip } = req.connection;
  if (clients.has(ip)) {
    res.status(403).send('<html><body><h1>403 Forbidden</h1></body></html>');
  } else {
    clients.add(ip);
    const { filename } = req.params;
    fs.access(filename, fs.constants.R_OK, (err) => {
      if (err) {
        res.status(404).send('<html><body><h1>404 Not Found</h1></body></html>');
      } else {
        fs.readFile(filename, (error, data) => {
          if (error) {
            res.status(500).send('<html><body><h1>500 Internal Server Error</h1></body></html>');
          } else {
            res.status(200).send(data);
            fs.unlink(filename, (unlinkError) => {
              if (unlinkError) {
                console.error(unlinkError);
              }
            });
          }
        });
      }
    });
  }
});

https.createServer(options, app).listen(443, () => {
  console.log('HTTPS file server listening on port 443');
});```

This implementation uses the fs, https, and express libraries to serve files over HTTPS. The express library is used to handle incoming HTTP requests and send HTTP responses.

The `clients` set is used to keep track of connected clients and ensure that each file is only served once to a single IP address. The `app.get` method is used to handle incoming GET requests and serve the requested file. If the file cannot be found, the function sends an error page with a 404 status code. If the connecting IP address has already received a file, the function sends an error page with a 403 status code.

